 \section EBUse Using the Energy Balance Model Plug-in

 \subsection EBPrimData Input Variables

 Inputs to the model are set by creating primitive variable data in the usual way. If a variable needed for a model input has not been create in the Context, the default value is assumed.

 \subsection EBRad Input Radiative Bands

 In order for the model to calculate the absorbed all-wave radiation flux, it needs to know the names of all radiation bands that were added to the radiation model. This is done using the function \ref EnergyBalanceModel::addRadiationBand() "addRadiationBand()". In the following example, assume we have three radiative wavebands "PAR", "NIR", and "LW".

 ```
  // Initialize the Model
  EnergyBalanceModel energybalance(&context);

  energybalance.addRadiationBand("PAR");
  energybalance.addRadiationBand("NIR");
  energybalance.addRadiationBand("LW");
 ```

 \subsection EBGPUControl GPU Acceleration Control

 The Energy Balance plugin automatically detects GPU availability at runtime and uses GPU acceleration when available. However, you can manually control whether to use GPU or CPU execution:

 ```
  EnergyBalanceModel energybalance(&context);

  // Force CPU mode (even if GPU is available)
  energybalance.disableGPUAcceleration();

  // Enable GPU if available
  energybalance.enableGPUAcceleration();

  // Check current mode
  if (energybalance.isGPUAccelerationEnabled()) {
      std::cout << "Using GPU acceleration" << std::endl;
  } else {
      std::cout << "Using CPU (OpenMP) implementation" << std::endl;
  }
 ```

 <b>Note:</b> These methods are only available when the plugin is compiled with CUDA support (HELIOS_CUDA_AVAILABLE defined). For CPU-only builds, these methods are not available and the plugin always uses OpenMP.

 <b>When to use CPU mode:</b>
 <ul>
 <li>Benchmarks show CPU implementation is faster than GPU for workloads < 1M primitives</li>
 <li>Testing and validation (ensure consistent results across platforms)</li>
 <li>Debugging (simpler execution path)</li>
 <li>Systems where GPU is shared with visualization</li>
 </ul>

 \subsection EBblc Boundary-layer Conductance Model

 The primitive boundary-layer conductance can either be set using the \ref BLConductanceModel plug-in, or using the default model which is the Polhausen equation. Note also that custom conductance values can also be provided by setting the value of primitive data labeled 'boundarylayer_conductance', which overrides the computed model value.

 It is also important to note that, by default, the length scale used to calculate the boundary-layer conductance is taken to be the square root of the primitive surface area. If the size of the object is different from the size of the primitive, then it is important to manually set the length scale to be the size of the object, as this is the relevant scale for boundary-layer development.

 The default boundary-layer conductance is calculated as

 \f$g_H = 0.135 n_s\sqrt{\frac{U}{L}}\f$,

 where \f$U\f$ is the wind speed just outside of the primitive boundary-layer, and \f$L\f$ is the characteristic length/dimension of the object that the primitive belongs to. For a leaf consisting of a single primitive, \f$L\f$ could be assumed to be the length of the primitive. \f$n_s\f$ is the number of primitive faces, which is determined by the value of primitive data "twosided_flag" (twosided\_flag=0 is single-sided and \f$n_s=1\f$, twosided\_flag=1 is two-sided and \f$n_s=2\f$).

 \subsection EBgm Moisture Conductance

 For surfaces that are not completely dry or completely saturated with water, availability of water at the surface determines the rate of moisture transfer from the surface. This is represented by the surface moisture conductance \f$g_S\f$, and corresponds to the conductance between the sub-surface air spaces and the surface.

  For a dry surface, \f$g_S=0\f$ and thus there is no moisture transfer at the surface. For a surface that is saturated with water (e.g., a lake, puddle), \f$g_S\rightarrow\infty\f$ as the supply of water at the surface is theoretically infinite.

  For surfaces such as leaves and the soil in which surface moisture is present but limited, \f$g_S\f$ will have some intermediate value typically on the order of 0.1 mol air/m<sup>2</sup>-s.

  For leaves, \f$g_S\f$ corresponds to the conductance to water vapor between the intercellular (sub-stomatal) air spaces and the surface of the leaf, which we call the stomatal conductance. When stomata are closed, \f$g_S\rightarrow 0\f$, as water transfer is restricted by the stomata.

  For the soil surface, the physical interpretation of \f$g_S\f$ is slightly less intuitive, and corresponds to the rate at which water vapor can diffuse from just below the soil to the surface. This is impacted by the soil texture, and the "tortuosity" of the path water vapor must take through the soil, among other factors.

   Once the water vapor has diffused to the surface, the rate of transfer is then determined by the surface boundary-layer conductance. The sub-surface conductance and boundary-layer conductance act in serial, and can be combined to yield an overall moisture conductance between the sub-surface air spaces and the outside of the boundary layer according to

   \f$g_M = 1.08g_Hg_S\left[\dfrac{\zeta}{1.08g_H+g_S\zeta}+\dfrac{(1-\zeta)}{1.08g_H+g_S(1-\zeta)}\right]\f$,

   where \f$g_H\f$ is the boundary-layer conductance to heat, and \f$1.08g_H\f$ gives the boundary-layer conductance to moisture considering the differences in diffusivity between water vapor and heat.

   \f$\zeta=\dfrac{D_{upper}}{D_{lower}+D_{upper}}\f$

   is the stomatal sidedness, which is the ratio of the stomatal density of the upper leaf surface to the sum of the upper and lower leaf surface densities, which is set by the primitive data value "stomatal_sidedness". For leaves, \f$\zeta=0\f$ corresponds to hypostomatous leaves (stomata on one side), and \f$\zeta=0.5\f$ to amphistomatous leaves (stomata equally on both sides). It is important to note that if \f$n_s=1\f$, then the value of \f$\zeta\f$ will be overridden and set to 0.

   \subsection EBRun Running the Steady-State Model

   The model is run assuming steady-state conditions (heat storage term is zero) using the \ref EnergyBalanceModel::run() function, which will run the model for all primitives in the Context if no argument is given, or will run the model for a subset of primitives if a vector of UUIDs is given as the argument.

   <table>
      <caption>Functions to run the steady-state energy balance model.</caption>
      <tr><th>Model Run Function <th>Description
      <tr><td>\ref EnergyBalanceModel::run( void ) <td>Run the model for all primitives in the Context.
      <tr><td>\ref EnergyBalanceModel::run( const std::vector<uint> &UUIDs ) <td>Run the model for a select set of primitives in the Context, which are specified by a vector of their UUIDs.
   </table>

   <p> <br><br> </p>

   ~~~~~~{.cpp}
    #include "EnergyBalanceModel.h"
    using namespace helios;

    int main(){

        // Initialize the Context
        Context context;

        // Add `Patch' primitive
        vec3 center = make_vec3(0,0,1);
        vec2 size = make_vec2(1,1);
        uint UUID = context.addPatch( center, size );

        EnergyBalanceModel energybalance(&context);

        energybalance.addRadiationBand("PAR");
        energybalance.addRadiationBand("NIR");
        energybalance.addRadiationBand("LW");

        energybalance.run();

        return 0;
    }
   ~~~~~~

 \subsubsection EBRunUS Unsteady model with heat storage

 Additional functions are available to run the unsteady energy balance model with heat storage. If the timestep argument "dt" is passed with value greater than 0, and the heat capacity \f$C_p\f$ is greater than 0, the unsteady energy balance equation will be applied with the heat storage term included. The following equation is then solved for the surface temperature at time \f$t+\Delta t\f$

 \f[ R-n_s\varepsilon\sigma T_s(t+\Delta t)^4 - c_p g_H \left( T_s(t+\Delta t)-T_a \right) - \lambda g_M \left(\frac{e_s(T_s(t+\Delta t))f_s-e_s(T_a)h}{p_{atm}}\right)-Q_{other} + C_p\dfrac{T_s(t+\Delta t) - T_s(t)}{\Delta t}.\f]

 The timestep should be chosen such that it is significantly smaller than the characteristic time constant of heat storage.

 Functions for running the unsteady energy balance model are listed below.

 <table>
 <caption>Functions to run the unsteady energy balance model.</caption>
 <tr><th>Model Run Function <th>Description
 <tr><td>\ref EnergyBalanceModel::run( float dt ) <td>Run the model for all primitives in the Context and advance in time by dt seconds.
 <tr><td>\ref EnergyBalanceModel::run( const std::vector<uint> &UUIDs, float dt ) <td>Run the model for a select set of primitives in the Context, which are specified by a vector of their UUIDs, and advance in time by dt seconds.
 </table>

 Note that you can run the steady-state model for some primitives but run the unsteady model for others simply by changing the value of the heat capacity primitive data. This is illustrated in the code example below.

 <p> <br><br> </p>

 ~~~~~~{.cpp}
 #include "EnergyBalanceModel.h"
 using namespace helios;

 int main(){

    // Initialize the Context
    Context context;

    // Add one 'Patch' primitive
    vec3 center = make_vec3(0,0,1);
    vec2 size = make_vec2(1,1);
    uint UUID1 = context.addPatch( center, size );

    // Add a second `Patch' primitive
    center = make_vec3(2,0,1);
    uint UUID2 = context.addPatch( center, size );

    // Set the heat capacity of the first patch so that the unsteady energy balance model will be run
    float Cp = 10000; //J/m^2-oC
    context.setPrimitiveData( UUID1, "heat_capacity", Cp );

    EnergyBalanceModel energybalance(&context);

    energybalance.addRadiationBand("PAR");
    energybalance.addRadiationBand("NIR");
    energybalance.addRadiationBand("LW");

    float dt = 180;
    energybalance.run( dt );

    return 0;
 }
 ~~~~~~


 \section EBCanopyAirspace Canopy Airspace Model

 By default, the energy balance model treats the air temperature and humidity surrounding each primitive as a prescribed input, usually set to a value measured by a weather station. This means that the canopy cannot influence the air that drives its own transpiration: no matter how much the canopy transpires, the air it sits in remains at the measured temperature and humidity.

 The canopy airspace model relaxes this assumption by solving for the within-canopy (aerodynamic) air temperature \f$T_{ac}\f$ and water vapor mole fraction \f$x_{ac}\f$ that surround leaves. These are intermediate between the prevailing above-canopy conditions and the canopy itself, and they respond to the sensible and latent heat the canopy releases. This allows the canopy to cool and humidify the air within it, which in turn feeds back on transpiration.

 The model is enabled with \ref EnergyBalanceModel::enableCanopyAirspaceModel(). Once enabled, \ref EnergyBalanceModel::run() solves the coupled system rather than performing a single surface energy balance pass.

 \subsection EBCanopyAirspaceVsAir Relationship to the Air Energy Balance Model

 This model should not be confused with \ref EnergyBalanceModel::enableAirEnergyBalance(), which is a separate and independent model. The two differ in their upper boundary condition:

 <table>
 <tr><th>Model<th>Upper boundary<th>Solution
 <tr><td>\ref EnergyBalanceModel::enableAirEnergyBalance()<td>Prognostic atmospheric boundary layer, evolved in time<td>Time integration
 <tr><td>\ref EnergyBalanceModel::enableCanopyAirspaceModel()<td>Measured above-canopy air temperature and humidity, held fixed<td>Steady state
 </table>

 The air energy balance model carries an above-canopy energy reservoir, which implicitly assumes a horizontally infinite canopy. The canopy airspace model instead imposes measured conditions at the canopy top, so the surrounding atmosphere is unaffected by the simulated canopy. This is the appropriate choice for a canopy of limited horizontal extent subject to advection and mesoscale forcing, such as an orchard block, where the above-canopy air is set by the wider landscape rather than by the modeled canopy.

 Because it solves for a steady state, the canopy airspace model cannot be combined with the unsteady form of \ref EnergyBalanceModel::run() that takes a timestep argument; doing so produces an error.

 \subsection EBCanopyAirspaceTheory Theory

 The canopy airspace is divided into \f$l\f$ vertical layers, each containing an equal increment of leaf area index. Each layer exchanges sensible heat and water vapor with the leaves it contains, with the layers immediately above and below it, and, for the uppermost and lowermost layers, with the above-canopy reference air and the soil surface respectively.

 The air temperature of layer \f$j\f$ is obtained by requiring that sensible heat entering the layer balances that leaving it:

 \f[ T_{ac,j} = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(g_{bl,i}\frac{A_i}{A_g}T_i\right) + \frac{T_{ac,j-1}}{R_{ac,j-1}} + \frac{T_{ac,j+1}}{R_{ac,j+1}}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(g_{bl,i}\frac{A_i}{A_g}\right) + \frac{1}{R_{ac,j-1}} + \frac{1}{R_{ac,j+1}}}, \f]

 where \f$T_i\f$ is the surface temperature of leaf \f$i\f$, \f$g_{bl,i}\f$ is its boundary-layer conductance, \f$A_i\f$ is its one-sided area, and \f$A_g\f$ is the ground area of the canopy. Leaves are weighted by their boundary-layer conductance because that determines their relative contribution to sensible heat exchange with the surrounding air.

 An analogous expression governs the water vapor mole fraction of each layer, with two differences. First, leaves are weighted by their total conductance to moisture \f$g_M\f$ (see \ref EBgm), which places the stomata in series with the boundary layer, rather than by \f$g_{bl}\f$ alone. This is the same expression used to compute the leaf latent heat flux, including the partition between stomata-bearing faces, so that the water weighted into the airspace matches the water the leaf actually transpires. A leaf with closed stomata therefore contributes no water vapor to the airspace. Second, the substomatal airspace is assumed to be saturated at the leaf surface temperature, so each leaf acts as a source at \f$e_s(T_i)\f$. Evaporation from the soil surface is assumed to be zero, and the soil exchanges sensible heat with the lowest layer through the bare-ground conductance \f$g_{Hg}=0.166+0.5U\f$ of Kustas and Norman (1999), evaluated at the wind speed at the soil surface.

 Turbulent exchange between adjacent layers is represented by an eddy diffusivity for heat, \f$K\f$, so that the conductance across the boundary between two layers is \f$\rho K/\Delta z\f$, where \f$\Delta z\f$ is the distance between the layer midpoints. Turbulence is strongly damped with depth into the canopy, so \f$K\f$ decays exponentially from its value at the canopy top with the same attenuation coefficient used for the wind profile:

 \f[ K(z) = \kappa u_* (h_c-d)\exp\left[-a\left(1-z/h_c\right)\right]. \f]

 Because the conductance is formed from a diffusivity divided by a real distance, the number of layers acts as a numerical resolution rather than as a physical parameter: refining it resolves the profile more finely without changing the transport it represents. Note that turbulent eddies carry heat and water vapor alike, so the layer-to-layer conductances are the same for both; the ratio of vapor to heat diffusivity applies only to the molecular boundary layer at the leaf surface.

 Setting the number of layers to 1 gives a single within-canopy node whose only neighbors are the reference air above and the soil surface below.

 Because leaf temperature depends on the airspace state and the airspace state depends on leaf temperature, the two are solved iteratively: the surface energy balance and the airspace solution are alternated until the layer air temperatures stop changing. Convergence criteria may be adjusted with \ref EnergyBalanceModel::setCanopyAirspaceConvergence().

 \subsection EBCanopyAirspaceRa Aerodynamic Resistance and Wind Profile

 The resistance between the canopy top and the reference height follows Perrier (1975):

 \f[ r_a = \frac{\ln\left[\frac{z_m-d}{z_{oM}}\right]\ln\left[\frac{z_H-d}{h_c-d}\right]}{\kappa^2 u}, \f]

 where \f$z_m\f$ and \f$z_H\f$ are the measurement heights of wind speed and of temperature and humidity, \f$h_c\f$ is the canopy height, \f$\kappa\f$ is the von Karman constant, and \f$u\f$ is the wind speed at the reference height. The roughness length governing momentum transfer \f$z_{oM}\f$ and the zero-plane displacement height \f$d\f$ are computed from the canopy height and leaf area index following Perrier (1982):

 \f[ z_{oM} = h_c e^{-\mathrm{LAI}/2}\left(1-e^{-\mathrm{LAI}/2}\right), \qquad d = h_c\left[1-\frac{2}{\mathrm{LAI}}\left(1-e^{-\mathrm{LAI}/2}\right)\right]. \f]

 Wind speed decreases with depth into the canopy, and neglecting this attenuation overestimates the mean wind speed experienced by leaves and hence overestimates transpiration. The model therefore writes primitive data "wind_speed" for every canopy primitive using the exponential within-canopy profile of Cionco (1972):

 \f[ U(z) = U(h_c)\exp\left[-a\left(1-z/h_c\right)\right], \f]

 where \f$z\f$ is the height of the primitive above the base of the canopy. The attenuation coefficient \f$a\f$ is taken as one half of the leaf area index, following Frehner et al.; note that this relation is an empirical choice rather than part of Cionco's original formulation, and that reported values of \f$a\f$ for orchards can be considerably smaller. The resulting wind speed is used in the boundary-layer conductance calculation for each primitive (see \ref EBblc).

 \subsection EBCanopyAirspaceUse Using the Canopy Airspace Model

 The model requires the canopy primitives, the ground primitives forming the soil node, the canopy height, the reference height at which above-canopy conditions are measured, the leaf area index, and the number of layers. The ground primitive set may be empty, in which case there is no exchange with the soil surface; when it is not empty, every ground primitive must carry primitive data "temperature".

 The leaf area index must be at least 0.5, below which the Perrier roughness relations are not valid, and the reference height must lie far enough above the canopy for the aerodynamic resistance to be defined. The number of layers is limited by the geometry: a layer that ends up containing no leaf area is an error, so a canopy resolved by few primitives cannot support many layers. Both the aerodynamic resistance and the roughness relations assume neutral stability.

 The above-canopy boundary condition is read from global data "air_temperature_reference", "air_humidity_reference", and "wind_speed_reference".

 <table>
 <caption>Global data written by the canopy airspace model.</caption>
 <tr><th>Global data<th>Type<th>Description
 <tr><td>canopy_air_temperature<td>float<td>Mean within-canopy air temperature across all layers (Kelvin).
 <tr><td>canopy_air_humidity<td>float<td>Mean within-canopy air relative humidity across all layers.
 <tr><td>canopy_air_temperature_layers<td>vector&lt;float&gt;<td>Air temperature of each layer (Kelvin), ordered from the bottom layer to the top layer.
 <tr><td>canopy_air_humidity_layers<td>vector&lt;float&gt;<td>Relative humidity of each layer, ordered from the bottom layer to the top layer.
 <tr><td>aerodynamic_resistance<td>float<td>Aerodynamic resistance between the canopy top and the reference height (s/m).
 <tr><td>canopy_airspace_iterations<td>uint<td>Number of iterations performed.
 </table>

 The model writes primitive data "air_temperature", "air_humidity", and "wind_speed" for all canopy primitives, and adds "boundarylayer_conductance_out" to the optional outputs because the airspace solution requires it.

 The ground primitives are part of the solution rather than a fixed boundary condition. They are driven by the lowest canopy layer's air temperature and humidity and by the wind speed at the soil surface, all of which the model writes to their primitive data, and the heat they release returns to that same layer. Every primitive given to this function, canopy and ground alike, must therefore also be included in the primitives passed to \ref EnergyBalanceModel::run(); otherwise its surface temperature would never be updated to correspond to the air it exchanges with, and the model raises an error.

 <p> <br><br> </p>

 ~~~~~~{.cpp}
 #include "EnergyBalanceModel.h"
 using namespace helios;

 int main(){

    Context context;

    // ... build the canopy and ground geometry, and run the radiation model ...

    std::vector<uint> leaf_UUIDs = context.filterPrimitivesByData( context.getAllUUIDs(), "object_label", "leaf" );
    std::vector<uint> ground_UUIDs = context.filterPrimitivesByData( context.getAllUUIDs(), "object_label", "ground" );

    // Above-canopy conditions measured at the weather station
    context.setGlobalData( "air_temperature_reference", 305.f );  // Kelvin
    context.setGlobalData( "air_humidity_reference", 0.4f );      // fraction
    context.setGlobalData( "wind_speed_reference", 2.f );         // m/s

    EnergyBalanceModel energybalance(&context);

    energybalance.addRadiationBand("PAR");
    energybalance.addRadiationBand("NIR");
    energybalance.addRadiationBand("LW");

    float canopy_height = 4.f;      // m
    float reference_height = 6.f;   // m, height of the weather station
    float LAI = 3.f;                // m^2 leaf area per m^2 ground area
    uint num_layers = 10;

    energybalance.enableCanopyAirspaceModel( leaf_UUIDs, ground_UUIDs, canopy_height, reference_height, LAI, num_layers );

    energybalance.run();

    // Retrieve the resolved vertical profile of within-canopy air temperature
    std::vector<float> T_layers;
    context.getGlobalData( "canopy_air_temperature_layers", T_layers );

    return 0;
 }
 ~~~~~~


*/
